## Lab Objectives

- Learn how to place devices in Cisco Packet Tracer
- Create two separate branch networks
- Connect computers and servers to switches
- Connect each branch to a router and firewall
- Represent communication across the internet
- Understand the basic purpose of routers, switches, and firewalls
- Represent an external attacker on an untrusted network

## Topology Components

### New York Branch

The New York branch contains:

- PC1
- PC2
- SW1
- R1
- FW1

The two computers connect to SW1. The switch connects the local devices to R1, which connects toward the firewall and internet.

### Internet Segment

The center of the diagram represents the internet or wide-area network connection between the two branches.

An attacker device is connected to this segment to represent an external untrusted system.

### Tokyo Branch

The Tokyo branch contains:

- SRV1
- SRV2
- SW2
- R2
- FW2

The two servers connect to SW2. The switch connects the servers to the branch firewall and router infrastructure.

## Device Inventory

| Device | Name | Quantity | Purpose |
|---|---|---:|---|
| PC | PC1, PC2 | 2 | Represents New York employees |
| Switch | SW1, SW2 | 2 | Connects devices inside each local network |
| Router | R1, R2 | 2 | Routes traffic between networks |
| Firewall | FW1, FW2 | 2 | Protects each branch from untrusted traffic |
| Server | SRV1, SRV2 | 2 | Represents services hosted in the Tokyo branch |
| Internet router | The Internet | 1 | Represents the public network between branches |
| Attacker computer | Attacker | 1 | Represents an external security threat |

